Chapter 27 — Part 1
Curtis never made it back to the suite. After the alley he turned the other way, helmet under his arm, and took the late train out toward the tree line. The forest was waiting. He stepped off the path again and disappeared into the dark to train.
Back at the building, Hina and Yui stood outside his door. The lights were off. No music. No movement under the gap. Hina knocked once, soft.
“Maybe he’s sleeping?”
Yui already had her phone out. “Text him!”
Hina pulled hers up too. Both of them stared at the dark suite, the worry starting to edge past the usual warmth. The messages went out. No reply came. The hallway stayed quiet while the two girls waited, and somewhere past the city the low, steady pull in Curtis’s chest had gone sharp again among the trees.
Curtis’s phone buzzed in his pocket while he reset his stance between the trees. He checked it under the dim canopy light.
Hina:
You okay? Lights are off.
Yui:
Answer or we’re breaking in.
He typed with his thumb, breath still even from the last exchange with the dark.
Curtis:
I won’t be home tonight. Don’t wait up.
The photo arrived a second later. Both of them on the couch, faces covered by their hands, shirts yanked up. Full breasts out, nipples tight, the shot angled just enough to be deliberate. Free Love didn’t need him in the room.
Curtis stared at it for a beat. The corner of his mouth twitched.
Curtis:
Damn. Guess I should’ve gone home. Next time. Training comes first.
He locked the phone, set it on a root, and turned back to the shape watching him from the trees. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed sharp. The photo sat unanswered in the way that mattered. He raised his hands again and kept working while the city and the two girls waited somewhere behind him
In the apartment Hina and Yui stared at the reply. The photo was still open on Hina’s screen. Yui snorted, half-laugh, half-frustrated.
“Training comes first. Of course it does.” She dropped her shirt back down. “He’s in the murder woods again, isn’t he.”
“Probably.” Hina locked her phone, cheeks still hot. “At least he answered.”
They didn’t break in. They turned off the living-room light and went to their own rooms, the want still sitting under their skin, the knowledge that he was somewhere in the dark choosing the forest over the bed they’d left open.
Out past the city Curtis moved through another exchange. The shape in the trees tested him—joints clicking, strikes that would have broken ordinary bone. He met them. Air pockets. A copied burst of flame that lit the moss for a second. Permeation when a blow needed to miss. He didn’t use everything. Just enough to stay sharp. Sweat ran under the coat. The cut on his lip had opened again. He didn’t stop.
Hours later the first gray light started filtering through the canopy. Curtis stepped back onto the path, helmet under his arm, jacket torn in two new places. He looked toward the city, then at the dark behind him.
“Same time tomorrow,” he told the trees.
Nothing answered. He started the long walk toward the first train, the low, steady pull in his chest finally settling quiet, while the two girls slept in the apartment above his empty suite and the Festival waited thirteen days away.
Morning had fully arrived by the time Curtis reached the building. Yui was on the balcony with a mug, hair still messy, when she spotted him coming up the side street. She leaned on the rail.
His clothes were tattered. The coat from last night was gone; he was in the torn compression gear again, jacket missing, shirt ripped at the shoulder and hem. Dirt and dried blood marked the fabric. The cut on his lip had reopened and crusted. He walked like the night hadn’t ended, red cap nowhere in sight, braids loose.
Yui’s mouth opened, then closed. She set the mug down and called over the rail, voice carrying.
“Curtis! You look like the forest won.”
He looked up, heterochromia catching the light, and gave a short wave with the scarred hand. “It didn’t. I’m fine.”
She didn’t look convinced. She was already turning for the door, bare feet on the balcony tile, while he reached the entrance below and the low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet under the morning sun.
Yui met him at the stairwell door, mug abandoned upstairs. She looked him over—ripped shirt, dried blood at the lip, the way he was still carrying the night in his shoulders—and didn’t bother with a joke.
“You’re not fine. You’re upright. There’s a difference.” She stepped aside so he could pass. “Hina’s still asleep. I was about to wake her if you didn’t show.”
Curtis nodded and started down toward his suite. “Told you not to wait up.”
“We didn’t. We just worried.” She followed him the half-flight. “Was it the same thing as yesterday?”
“Same forest. Different hours.” He unlocked the door. The suite was dark and untouched. “I’m gonna shower. Then sleep. Shift’s not till later.”
Yui lingered in the doorway. The usual flush was there, but it sat under the concern this time. “Text when you’re actually in the bed. So we know you didn’t phase through the floor and keep walking.”
“Will do.” He glanced back, expression even. “Thanks for checking.”
She gave a small nod and headed upstairs. Curtis shut the door, stripped the ruined shirt, and turned the shower on. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet while the hot water hit the new bruises and the city morning kept moving outside the high window.
Curtis smirked as he stepped fully into the suite and shut the door. The thought came easy, low under his breath.
“Irony to what she said. I bet she doesn’t know I have Permeation. From Mirio. I think that was his name.”
He tossed the ruined shirt toward the corner and headed for the shower. The water was already running. He didn’t phase through anything on the way. Just walked. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet while the steam started to fill the small bathroom and the joke sat with him, unshared.
Hina woke to the smell of coffee and Yui already dressed, sitting on the edge of the bed.
“He came home,” Yui said before Hina could ask. “About twenty minutes ago. Looked like the forest used him as a punching bag. Torn shirt, blood on his lip again, walking like he hadn’t slept. He said he was fine and went to shower.”
Hina sat up, frown immediate. “Again? Janice is going to be pissed. She already told him no more marks before shifts.” She rubbed her face. “If he shows up tonight looking like that, she’s putting him on glass duty or sending him home. And he needs the hours.”
Yui shrugged, but the worry was there. “He said the shift’s later. Maybe the lip closes. Maybe he listens for once.” She handed Hina the second mug. “Or maybe we both pretend we didn’t see how bad it was and let Janice yell at him herself.”
Hina took the coffee, still frowning. “She’s gonna yell either way.”
They sat with it. Downstairs the shower had already stopped. The suite was quiet. The Festival was still days away, and the cut on Curtis’s lip was already becoming a pattern neither of them knew how to break.
Evening settled over the building. Curtis was awake, stretched out on the bed with his back against the wall, an old acoustic guitar across his lap. The lip had closed enough that it didn’t split when he hummed. He picked through a slow, unfinished progression—nothing from the club set, just something that had been sitting in his head since the forest. The suite lights were low. The soundproof panels caught most of the sound.
The knock was soft. Three taps. He set the guitar aside and opened the door.
Hina stood there in casual clothes, hair down, a small container in her hands. She took in the fresh shirt, the lack of new blood, and some of the tension left her shoulders. “You’re up. Yui said you looked rough this morning. I brought food. Actual food. Not whatever you cook when you’re half-dead.”
“Appreciate it.” He stepped aside. She came in, set the container on the counter, and glanced at the guitar.
“Didn’t know you played outside of work.”
“Keeps the hands busy.” He leaned against the wall, arms loose. “Janice still mad about the lip?”
“She hasn’t seen tonight’s version yet.” Hina opened the container—rice, grilled chicken, vegetables. “If you walk in clean she might only yell a little. If it opens again she’s sticking you on glass duty. She meant it.”
Curtis nodded. He took the container and ate standing up, unhurried. Hina stayed near the counter, watching him more than the room. The usual warmth was there, but it sat under the practical worry.
“You don’t have to keep going out there,” she said after a minute. “The forest. Whatever’s in it. The Festival’s in less than two weeks. You already got convinced to enter. You don’t have to show up looking like you lost a fight every morning.”
“I’m not losing.” His tone stayed even. “And I’m not training for the Festival. I’m training because I always do. The stage doesn’t change that.” He set the half-empty container down. “Yui okay? She saw me come in.”
“She’s fine. Worried. She doesn’t work tonight, so she gets to sit with it.” Hina’s mouth twitched. “I do work. So I get to watch Janice decide if your face is customer-ready.”
Curtis huffed a short laugh. “Tell her I’ll ice it on the train. And that the forest didn’t win.”
Hina stepped closer. She didn’t touch the lip. She just looked at it, then at him. “Text me when you’re on the way. So I know you actually left the suite and didn’t phase through the back wall to go train again.”
“I don’t phase through walls.” He met her eyes. “But I’ll text.”
She nodded, satisfied enough. They stood in the quiet suite a moment longer, the guitar leaning against the bed, the food half-eaten, the night shift waiting. Outside, the city kept moving. Down the hall Yui was probably still on the balcony. Hina stayed until he finished eating, then headed for the door with the empty container.
“See you at Lumen,” she said. “Try not to add anything new before then.”
“No promises.” The corner of his mouth twitched. “But I’ll aim.”
She left. The door clicked shut. Curtis picked the guitar back up, sat on the edge of the bed, and played the same slow progression until it was time to change into the work uniform. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet. The forest could wait until after the shift. For now the only thing that needed handling was a lip that had better stay closed under the club lights.
Curtis stepped through Lumen’s side door in the work uniform—black shirt, red tie, white suspenders, white pants. The lip had been iced. It still showed. A thin, dark line that hadn’t fully sealed. He clocked in and headed for the floor.
Janice spotted him from the bar before he made it ten steps. She set the glass she was polishing down hard enough to click.
“Beardy… oh for Pete’s sake. Again?”
She came around the counter, eyes narrowed on the cut. Tatsuya glanced over from the reservation tablet but didn’t intervene yet. A couple of early regulars at the bar were already looking.
“I iced it,” Curtis said, even. “It’s closed.”
“It’s visible.” Janice tilted his chin with two fingers, checking under the light the same way she had the night before. “I told you. Customers treat marks like invitations. We spent half of last night redirecting hands. I’m not doing that again on a Friday.” She released him and crossed her arms. “You’re on bar support until it fades. No tables. You pour, you restock, you stay where I can see you. Hina can cover your usual section.”
Hina, already tying her apron near the staff door, shot him a look that was half “I told you” and half worry. Curtis nodded once.
“Understood.”
“And if it opens,” Janice added, “you’re done for the night. No arguments.” She pointed toward the back. “Ice pack. Five minutes. Then you’re behind the bar.”
He went. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet while the club lights warmed up and Janice muttered something about forests and insurance under her breath. The shift would be quieter than usual. The cut had made sure of that.
Tatsuya came around the bar during the first lull. He set a clean glass down and leaned on the counter, eyes on the cut.
“Damn. How do you train? Until you collapse?”
Curtis wiped the bar top, expression even. He nodded. “Well, yeah. It’s how I always trained.”
Tatsuya’s brow pulled. “That’s not funny.”
“I’m not joking.” Curtis rinsed the rag. “You stop when the thing you’re fighting stops. Or when you can’t move. Whichever comes first. Forest doesn’t have a bell.”
Tatsuya studied him for a second. The usual calm manager face had shifted into something closer to concern. “You’re in a hero school and working here. You don’t get to treat your body like it’s disposable between both. Janice already benched you. If you show up tomorrow looking worse, she’s going to start cutting shifts. And I won’t argue with her.”
Curtis met his eyes. “Noted. I’ll keep the lip closed tonight. That’s the deal.”
“Make it more than tonight.” Tatsuya straightened. “Ice is in the well if you need it. And if a customer asks, you walked into a door. Don’t give them the forest version.”
He headed back to the floor. Curtis stayed behind the bar, pouring when the tickets came, the low, steady pull in his chest quiet while the club moved around him and the cut stayed shut under the lights. Hina glanced over from her section once, caught his eye, and didn’t smile. She just kept working.
The bar stayed steady. Curtis worked through the tickets—pours, ice, the occasional restock—while the floor hummed with its usual Friday noise. During a lull he started humming under his breath, low enough that it almost blended with the music from the speakers.
“Paper heart but I’m fireproof

Every scar just tells the truth

Thought I’d break when life got rough

Guess I wasn’t paper enough…”
It wasn’t loud. Just enough for the nearest regular at the bar to catch the melody and glance over. She smiled, soft, and didn’t interrupt. Hina passed behind him with a tray, caught the tail end of the line, and slowed for half a second. She didn’t comment. She just set the tray down, took the drinks he slid her, and kept moving, the warmth in her cheeks a little higher than the club lights explained.
Janice noticed from the far end of the bar. She didn’t shut it down. She just watched him for a moment, arms crossed, then went back to checking the night’s numbers. The humming faded when the next ticket printed. Curtis went back to pouring. The cut stayed closed. The low, steady pull in his chest remained quiet while the unfinished song sat in the air a little longer than the ice in the glasses.
The regular at the bar waited until he set her refill down. She tilted her head, smile curious.
“What was that song called? The one you were humming.”
Curtis wiped a ring of condensation off the wood. “It’s called Paper Heart.”
She pulled her phone out, thumbs already moving. A second later she frowned at the screen. “Um… I can’t find it on YouTube or Spotify.”
He chuckled, light. “That’s because I wrote it and never released it.”
Her smile deepened. The flush that had been sitting on her cheeks since she sat down warmed a little more. “Oh. A music artist.”
“Hardly.” He leaned on the bar, expression easy. “I just write what I feel. Most of it stays in my head or on a guitar in my room. Doesn’t need to go further than that.”
She rested her chin on her hand, still looking at him. “It was good. The part I heard. You should record it sometime. People would listen.”
“Maybe.” He slid a fresh napkin under her glass. “For now it’s just something to hum when the bar’s quiet. You need anything else?”
“Not yet.” She didn’t look away right away. Curtis went back to the next ticket. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet while the unfinished song and the woman who had asked about it both lingered at the end of the counter.
